---
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';
import Hero from '../components/sections/Hero.astro';
import TheProblem from '../components/sections/TheProblem.astro';
import WhoThisIsFor from '../components/sections/WhoThisIsFor.astro';
import SeeItRun from '../components/sections/SeeItRun.astro';
import CustomerOutcomes from '../components/sections/CustomerOutcomes.astro';
import TheYield from '../components/sections/TheYield.astro';
import NutritionFacts from '../components/sections/NutritionFacts.astro';
import PaperIn from '../components/sections/PaperIn.astro';
import Sage from '../components/sections/Sage.astro';
import TeamAccess from '../components/sections/TeamAccess.astro';
import TheOtherTools from '../components/sections/TheOtherTools.astro';
import BuiltForKitchens from '../components/sections/BuiltForKitchens.astro';
import StartHere from '../components/sections/StartHere.astro';
import StickyCta from '../components/StickyCta.astro';
---

<!--
  One claim per scroll stop.

  SEE IT RUN MOVED TO SECOND ON 2026-09-06, at the owner's request: the footage
  now runs before the page argues anything. The reader this page is written for
  arrives cold from an email and gives it under a minute, and the question that
  minute is spent on is "is this a real product". Everything below that line was
  written while the answer sat at the fourth stop.

  WHAT THIS VOIDS, stated rather than left standing. The 2026-08-21 measurement
  below traded 604px of recall distance for 1,258px of depth off the video; the
  video is now at the top of the scroll, so that trade no longer describes the
  page and its pixel figures are history, not the current geometry. The
  2026-08-23 note below argues that WhoThisIsFor has to settle "is this aimed at
  me" BEFORE the footage plays; the owner's decision reverses that ordering. The
  two argue-then-show stops keep their order relative to each other, so the
  problem still sets up the answers that mirror it one for one, and that pairing
  (the one thing the sections must not lose) is untouched.

  NO NEW MEASUREMENT WAS RUN for this move. It records a decision, not a number.
  If it is ever re-litigated, the figures to take are the ones the 2026-08-21
  note took, at 390x844 with every [data-reveal] forced revealed.

  KNOWN CONSEQUENCE, StickyCta, MEASURED at 390x844 on this build with every
  [data-reveal] forced revealed. The phone bar appears once #demo has scrolled
  off the top and hides once #start is in view. Its rule is "the stretch where
  the reader has no primary within reach", and that is still exactly the stretch
  it covers; the stretch is simply longer.

    Scroll range 30,488px. The bar now appears at 5,299px and hides at 27,446px:
    22,147px, or 72.6% of the scroll.

    Before the move it would have appeared at 8,745px on this same build, since
    the only thing that changed is that #demo no longer sits behind #problem
    (2,238px) and #who (1,208px). That is 18,701px, or 61.3%.

  So the bar was already carrying most of the page. The move adds 11.3 points,
  not a category change. The trigger is deliberately left on #demo: moving it to
  a later section would invent a threshold nobody measured, and the rule it
  implements has not changed.

  SeeItRun moved from fourth to third on 2026-08-21. SETTLED BY MEASUREMENT at
  390x844, so nobody has to re-litigate it:

    What it cost. TheProblem's first pain and its answer in CustomerOutcomes
    sat 942px apart before (1.12 viewports); they now sit 1,546px apart (1.83).
    The move cost 604px, or 0.72 viewports, of recall distance.

    What it bought. The proof video moved from 3,155px to 1,897px: 3.7 mobile
    viewports down to 2.2, a gain of 1.5.

  The pairing reads as 1:1 on paper, but at 390px it was never co-visible: at
  1.12 viewports the reader has already scrolled a full screen past "you quote
  from an old price" before "bring the real price back" appears. So the move
  made a non-adjacency somewhat worse rather than destroying an adjacency. A
  0.72-viewport loss on recall bought a 1.5-viewport gain on the one element
  that settles "is this real" for a cold-email skeptic. Bigger gain, softer
  loss.

  WhoThisIsFor added 2026-08-23 as the third stop. It sits AFTER the problem
  and BEFORE the proof video on purpose: the reader has just been shown their
  own week, the next question is whether the tool is aimed at someone like
  them, and that has to be settled before the footage plays or the proof lands
  on a reader unsure it is theirs. It costs SeeItRun some depth on the page,
  which is the same currency the measurement above spends; it is worth it
  because the misfit list is the section a cold-email skeptic trusts most, and
  a reader who has decided the page is not about them never reaches the video
  at all. It reuses the /compare no rows rather than making new claims (RC-44).

  If CustomerOutcomes is ever rewritten so its answers stop mirroring
  TheProblem's pains one for one, this trade is void and the order is worth
  re-measuring.

  TheYield returns to the page after sitting unimported. It carries the one
  argument nothing else here makes and no competitor makes either.

  PaperIn added 2026-08-23, eighth stop, sitting seventh. The page argued the
  loop and never said how prices get INTO it, which is the single capability
  meez and Parsley lead with and we had been silent about. It is placed after
  TheYield rather than earlier on purpose: both are mechanics under the loop,
  and putting an import section in front of the proof video would open the page
  on data entry instead of on the job. It is NOT a fifth CustomerOutcomes card,
  because that pairing has to stay 1:1 with TheProblem or the measurement above
  is void. Backed by RC-09 and RC-26; see the header of the component.

  ONE TREATMENT FOR THE SECTION-OPENING ROLE, 2026-08-26. Five of the nine
  stops opened straight on an h2 while three carried an amber eyebrow above it
  (WhoThisIsFor, TheYield, PaperIn). Same semantic role, two costumes, which is
  part of what a reader reported as "a lot of text". The eyebrow is now on all
  nine. Hero keeps its own kicker instead: page-opening is a different role from
  section-opening. The eyebrow is the cheapest device on the page - 31px and two
  or three words per section, no image, no new colour.

  WHAT THE MEASUREMENT SAID. Rendered at 390x844 and 1440x900 with every
  [data-reveal] forced revealed and reduced motion on, the page was NOT short of
  space. Outer isolation - a section's largest internal gap against the gap that
  follows it - ran 1.14 to 8.35 at 390, and the flat-reading stops were at the
  LOW end. Adding air was the one fix the numbers ruled out.

  What they pointed at instead was internal flatness. PaperIn held both records
  at once: the highest isolation on the page (8.35 at 390, 14.4 at 1440) and the
  lowest internal differentiation on it, a 20px largest internal gap. That is
  what produced the longest run of pixels holding no image and no rule at either
  width - 990px at 390 and 824px at 1440, both inside it. A section can be
  perfectly separated from its neighbours and still read as a wall.

  So the fix there was four ticket rules under the four doors, not another 2,000
  pixels of white space. After: 742px at 390, 682px at 1440 (which now falls in
  TheProblem, not here), isolation down to 2.91 and 5.05, page height up 4.3%
  and 3.3%. The full record is BRIEF-visual-density-2026-08-26.md section 5.

  VISUAL GUIDANCE PASS, 2026-08-29. The page had one picture in ten sections
  and nothing pointing down. Seven changes, none of them a new claim: the
  product on the first screen (Hero), the empty right column filled in
  TheProblem, WhoThisIsFor and TheOtherTools, a looked-up hand-off line at the
  foot of every stop (SectionHandoff over src/lib/stops.ts), four footage crops
  in CustomerOutcomes (RC-48), a phone-only bar carrying the one primary
  between the demo and the close (StickyCta), and scroll reveals limited to
  the first three viewports so capture tools stop seeing a blank page.
  Story: docs/stories/homepage-visual-guidance.story.md

  SAGE ADDED 2026-08-29 as the eighth stop, between PaperIn and TheOtherTools,
  at the owner's request. It is a mechanic, so it sits with the mechanics, and
  it precedes the rival beat because that beat's /compare link is where "an
  assistant" appears as a comparison row. It prints its status (src/lib/sage.ts)
  rather than implying one; RC-46 and RC-49 hold the conditions. Not a
  CustomerOutcomes card, for the 1:1 reason above. Story: docs/stories/sage.story.md

  TEAM & ACCESS ADDED 2026-08-29 after Sage. Sage introduces role-aware checks;
  the next question is who can carry protected work. The homepage names the
  three roles and the missing permission grid, then hands the diligence read to
  /features/team-and-access. Story: docs/stories/team-and-access.story.md

  NUTRITION FACTS ADDED 2026-08-29 as the seventh stop, after TheYield and
  before PaperIn, at the owner's request. Both are recipe-level mechanics and
  yield sets up the sentence this one continues (the plate's numbers come from
  the line). Shipped, so no badge; the print page moved "printed labels" from
  Coming to Yes the same day (RC-50). Story: docs/stories/nutrition-facts.story.md

  ============================================================================
  THE MOBILE BUDGET, 2026-09-09. Every note above this line records what a
  decision VOIDED. None of them records what it COST, and that is how the page
  got where it is. This one sets the number the next section has to answer to.
  Full working: BRIEF-mobile-view-2026-09-09.md.

  HOW IT HAPPENED. Four measurements of this page at 390x844, all in this repo:

    2026-08-26   10,251px   BRIEF-visual-density-2026-08-26.md section 5B
    2026-08-29  ~12,700px   the header of StickyCta.astro
    2026-09-06   30,488px   the StickyCta note above
    2026-09-09   31,999px   BRIEF-mobile-view-2026-09-09.md

  Nine sections became thirteen and the phone column tripled, roughly 18,000px
  of it inside the single week between 08-29 and 09-06. No section was wrong.
  Nobody was measuring the total, so nobody had to argue for it.

  WHAT IS AND IS NOT BROKEN, measured 2026-09-09 and worth stating so the next
  pass does not re-diagnose it. The reading rhythm is FINE: the longest stretch
  of the page holding no rule, no card edge, no marker and no image is under one
  viewport everywhere except the demo guide. The 2026-08-26 structural work held
  and the sections added since inherited it. There is no wall to break. What is
  wrong is one number and only one: 37.9 mobile viewports and, corrected on
  2026-09-09, 3,681 visible words. The 4,388 first recorded here counted the
  sr-only demo transcript (709 words), which no sighted reader sees. The phone
  reader now sees 3,288, because the demo guide is collapsed. Full reconciliation
  in BRIEF-copy-density-2026-09-09.md section 1. Either figure is too many for a
  page CLAUDE.md says has under a minute to be worth a demo, and that brief
  carries the cut.

  THE BUDGET. Two rules, both about the phone, because the phone is 1.45x the
  desktop column for the same words and the phone is where the reader is.

    1. 30,000px at 390x844 is the ceiling. Not a target to grow into - the page
       is under it by 2,400px today and that margin is the whole allowance.
    2. A new section names what it displaces. Not "this is worth adding", which
       every one of the thirteen was, but which pixels it is taking and from
       where. A section that cannot name them is asking for the ceiling to move,
       and moving the ceiling is its own argument, made here, in writing.

  WHAT WAS SPENT AGAINST IT. The demo guide became a phone-only disclosure on
  2026-09-09 (see the note in SeeItRun.astro), which returned roughly 2,400px.
  That is the only fold this page has: the guide is the one stop that is
  explicitly an alternative to something the reader was just given. Every other
  section carries an argument, and folding an argument is not a height saving,
  it is a quieter page. If the ceiling is ever breached again, the honest lever
  is editorial - fewer claims, not shorter sentences, because the sentences
  already average 10.5 words - and that is a story-content pass with its own
  tracker, not a structural one. That pass RAN on 2026-09-09:
  BRIEF-copy-density-2026-09-09.md, tracker
  docs/stories/homepage-copy-density.story.md. It took 325 words out for 1,109px
  back, landing the page at 27,979px and 33.2 mobile viewports, so this ceiling
  now has just over 2,000px of headroom. Desktop went 22,017 to 21,487.

  THAT PIXEL FIGURE IS A MEASURED RATE, not a derived one, and the difference is
  worth keeping. Section height over section words averages 5 to 8 px per word,
  but most of that height is headings, media, card chrome and gaps, none of which
  move when a sentence goes. Removed in the live DOM at 390x844, a word is worth
  2.8px when a sentence is trimmed and 4.47px when a whole paragraph goes. So
  cutting claims pays 60% better than tightening prose, and the honest planning
  rate is about 4.2. THE DELIVERED RATE WAS 3.41, lower again, because most of what
  the pass found was a duplicated sentence inside a paragraph that had to stay
  rather than a whole paragraph that could go. Plan at 3.4.

  AND THE CUT STOPPED SHORT OF ITS 690-WORD TARGET ON PURPOSE, at 325. Every word
  it removed was a claim the page was already making somewhere else, usually
  within a screen. The remaining 365 would have to come out of claims the page
  makes ONCE - Sage's six guardrails, nutrition's "What it is not" - which are the
  reason a skeptic believes the sections they sit in. Shorter and less believable
  is design principle 2 read backwards, so that trade is the owner's to make, not
  a density pass's. It is worth about 1,250px if ever taken.
-->
<Base>
	<Hero />
	<SeeItRun />
	<TheProblem />
	<WhoThisIsFor />
	<CustomerOutcomes />
	<TheYield />
	<NutritionFacts />
	<PaperIn />
	<Sage />
	<TeamAccess />
	<TheOtherTools />
	<BuiltForKitchens />
	<StartHere />
	<StickyCta />
</Base>
